The `distancePointDocValuesAndSource` method is missing an `else` clause after the `if (leftCount == 1 && rightCount == 1)` block. The nested `for` loops execute unconditionally, meaning when both counts are 1, the distance is computed twice (once in the `if` block, once in the loop). While this doesn't produce an incorrect result (the minimum of a value with itself is the same value), it is:
1. Inconsistent with the three sibling methods (`distanceSourceAndConstant`, `distanceSourceAndSource`, `distancePointDocValuesAndConstant`), all of which correctly use `if/else`.
2. A redundant computation on the common single-value path.
The fix should be:
```java
if (leftCount == 1 && rightCount == 1) {
    distance = distance(...);
} else {
    for (int i = 0; i < leftCount; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < rightCount; j++) {
            ...
        }
    }
}
```

